<!--
  A STIPULATION, NOT A FILING. Nothing here was measured. The figures exist to put one state of
  the model in front of a checker, and that state is AN ELIMINATION FILED AS A DERIVATION RATHER
  THAN AS A FIGURE.

    crew-a nameplate    10 shifts per week   a point
    crew-b nameplate    10 shifts per week   a point
    shared block        NOT STATED           filed as the output of `sharedParts`
    fused nameplate     [15, 17, 18]         the composer's own figure, standing alone

  `pm:StatedEliminatedQuantity` has three arms and this is the third. A composer may state how much
  was double counted, may decline with a typed reason, or may name the identity that computes it and
  leave the arithmetic to the receiver. Until this document existed the third arm was admitted by the
  grammar, carried by a column in the relational form, and filed by nothing, so every relation that
  reads an eliminated quantity had only ever seen two of the three.

  ⛔ WHAT IT COSTS, AND THAT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A DEFECT. Nothing here computes a filed
  derivation's value: `sharedParts` names an identity over the highest layers two or more of a
  fusion's parts reach, and these parts reach no common layer, so there is nothing for it to sum.
  So the nameplate sum is SUSPENDED, `eliminations/unsized.sqlc` is where that is recorded, and the
  composed nameplate above is the composer's word and not a figure any rule here can hold them to.
  The demand elimination beside it is stated at zero, so the DEMAND sum is owed and is exact: one
  fusion, one quantity suspended and one checked, which is what makes the suspension visible as a
  choice rather than as a silence.

  ⚠️ The composer files the composed total and derives only the correction. That is a posture and
  not a contradiction: they are saying take the total from me and take the overlap from the parts,
  rather than writing one number down twice.
-->
